  <h2>The technical side</h2>

  <p>
    My degree was as much programming as it was design. I have worked in
    Java across several courses and projects, done database design and SQL,
    built and used REST APIs, and worked with Docker, Git and CI as part of
    the coursework. My bachelor project and the projects around it were
    built, not just drawn.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is a foundation across the stack rather than a specialism in one part
    of it. In practice it means I can build a working prototype myself
    rather than hand over a picture of one, and that I understand what I am
    asking a developer for when I ask for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Teaching</h2>

  <p>
    In the autumn of 2024 I was an instructor on the Human Computer
    Interaction course at Aarhus University. I ran the exercise sessions for
    the students and graded their assignments. Explaining a method to
    someone who is meeting it for the first time is a good test of whether
    you understand it yourself.
  </p>

